0 Excavator (Scoop)
0 Name: model.ldr
0 Author: ian-spike-learning
0 !LICENSE Licensed as content of this repo; parts from LDraw library
0 !LDRCHECK GROUND 80
0 !LDRCHECK FRONT +Z
0 // The boom is a subassembly hanging on one turning shaft, which is what
0 // a boom IS. Declared so the hinge rule stops calling it a defect; the
0 // joint itself is still checked for engagement and collision.
0 !LDRCHECK PIVOT 3705.dat +X

0 // Geometry notes -- see builds/excavator/geometry.md for the writeup.
0 // +Y is DOWN. Front is +Z. Ground plane is y = +80.
0 // The Hub is the chassis, not cargo: everything bolts to its side holes.
0 // Hub spans x -70..70, y -80..0, z -110..110, with its BOTTOM at y=0.
0 // A beam or frame placed with the identity matrix has its holes running
0 // along its own Y. The matrix 0 1 0 -1 0 0 0 0 1 turns that onto world
0 // X, which is what every vertical side plate here uses.

0 // Step 1 -- the Hub. Everything else hangs off its two side faces.
1 16 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 45601.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 2 -- eight 2M pins into the Hub's side holes: two per corner, at
0 // y=-50 and y=-30, on the z=+-80 columns. Two rows and not one, because
0 // a single row would leave each post free to swing about it. Pins sit
0 // at x=+-70 so each is half in the Hub (50..70) and half proud (70..90).
1 0 -70 -50 -80 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 61332.dat
1 0 -70 -50 80 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 61332.dat
1 0 -70 -30 -80 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 61332.dat
1 0 -70 -30 80 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 61332.dat
1 0 70 -50 -80 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 61332.dat
1 0 70 -50 80 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 61332.dat
1 0 70 -30 -80 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 61332.dat
1 0 70 -30 80 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 61332.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 3 -- four axle posts, one at each corner. These are 5M beams
0 // stood UPRIGHT: 0 1 0 0 0 1 1 0 0 runs the beam's length along Y and
0 // still points its holes along X. That is the whole trick of this
0 // chassis -- a beam lying along Z can only ever offer one hole row, and
0 // this build needs three different heights out of one part: y=-50 and
0 // y=-30 to catch the Hub's pins, and y=10 for the wheel axle, which has
0 // to sit below the Hub's y=0 underside.
0 // Centre y=-30 puts holes at y = -70, -50, -30, -10, 10 and stops the
0 // post at y=-79, flush with the Hub's own top face.
1 26 -80 -30 -80 0 1 0 0 0 1 1 0 0 32316.dat
1 26 -80 -30 80 0 1 0 0 0 1 1 0 0 32316.dat
1 26 80 -30 -80 0 1 0 0 0 1 1 0 0 32316.dat
1 26 80 -30 80 0 1 0 0 0 1 1 0 0 32316.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 4 -- the two wheel axles. 12M axles run the full width at y=10,
0 // which is the first hole row BELOW the Hub's bottom face (y=0), so the
0 // axle clears the Hub by 4 LDU instead of trying to pass through it.
1 4 0 10 -80 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 3708.dat
1 4 0 10 80 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 3708.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 5 -- four free-rolling wheels. Native wheel axis is Z; the
0 // matrix 0 0 1 0 1 0 -1 0 0 maps it onto X. Radius 70 from an axle at
0 // y=10 puts the tread on the ground plane at y=80.
1 16 -107.5 10 -80 0 0 1 0 1 0 -1 0 0 39367.dat
1 16 107.5 10 -80 0 0 1 0 1 0 -1 0 0 39367.dat
1 16 -107.5 10 80 0 0 1 0 1 0 -1 0 0 39367.dat
1 16 107.5 10 80 0 0 1 0 1 0 -1 0 0 39367.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 6 -- four 2M pins into the Hub's FRONT face, on the x=+-40
0 // columns at y=-50 and y=-10. These carry the whole arm, so they take
0 // two rows rather than one. A pin's own shaft runs along ITS X, so
0 // pointing one down the model's Z takes 0 0 1 0 1 0 -1 0 0 -- with the
0 // identity it lies across the nose instead and joins nothing.
1 0 -40 -50 110 0 0 1 0 1 0 -1 0 0 61332.dat
1 0 -40 -10 110 0 0 1 0 1 0 -1 0 0 61332.dat
1 0 40 -50 110 0 0 1 0 1 0 -1 0 0 61332.dat
1 0 40 -10 110 0 0 1 0 1 0 -1 0 0 61332.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 7 -- two upright mounting beams on the Hub's nose. Matrix
0 // -1 0 0 0 0 1 0 1 0 stands a 5M beam on end with its holes facing Z,
0 // giving a vertical column of five holes at y = -70, -50, -30, -10, 10.
0 // The Large Motor's own mounting holes are 80 LDU apart, and -70 to +10
0 // is the only pair on this machine that far apart -- the Hub's front
0 // face spans just 40, which is why the motor cannot bolt straight to it.
1 26 -40 -30 120 -1 0 0 0 0 1 0 1 0 32316.dat
1 26 40 -30 120 -1 0 0 0 0 1 0 1 0 32316.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 8 -- two 2M pins standing proud of the right-hand upright at
0 // y=-70 and y=10, ready for the motor.
1 0 40 -70 130 0 0 1 0 1 0 -1 0 0 61332.dat
1 0 40 10 130 0 0 1 0 1 0 -1 0 0 61332.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 9 -- the Large Motor, output disc facing LEFT. Matrix
0 // 0 1 0 -1 0 0 0 0 1 sends local Y to +X, so the disc turns about X and
0 // the boom swings in the YZ plane. The body sits to the RIGHT of the
0 // disc (x -10..70), which is what leaves the whole left side clear for
0 // the boom to sweep past it.
1 16 40 -30 160 0 1 0 -1 0 0 0 0 1 54675.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 10 -- boom pivot axle and the boom itself. The 4M axle reaches
0 // from x=-70 through the boom plate and into the disc's bore at x=-10.
0 // The boom is a 7x11 frame on edge, NOT a plain beam. A beam offers one
0 // line of holes, all facing the same way; the bucket motor has to bolt
0 // to a face at right angles to the pivot, and only a frame carries both.
0 // On edge this one is a 140x220 slab at x -50..-30 with pivot holes
0 // facing X at y=-30, and a bottom edge at y=-20 whose holes face Y --
0 // that edge is what the bucket motor hangs from in step 12.
0 // Its rearmost pivot hole sits on the axle, so the arm runs forward to
0 // z=370 and sweeps down the left side of the Large Motor.
1 0 -30 -30 160 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 3705.dat
1 26 -40 -90 260 0 1 0 -1 0 0 0 0 1 39794.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 11 -- three 2M pins hanging below the boom's bottom edge. Matrix
0 // 0 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 -1 stands a pin on end so it runs along Y. Three, not
0 // two, because this joint carries the bucket motor's whole overhang.
1 0 -40 -20 180 0 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 -1 61332.dat
1 0 -40 -20 300 0 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 -1 61332.dat
1 0 -40 -20 340 0 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 -1 61332.dat
0 STEP

0 // Step 12 -- the Medium Angular Motor, slung UNDER the boom with its
0 // disc at the front left (x=-110, y=10, z=320). Matrix 0 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 -1
0 // reverses it along Z so the body runs BACK from the disc instead of
0 // forward: pointing it the other way would put the bucket halfway along
0 // the arm with 150 LDU of motor sticking out past it.
1 16 -60 10 320 0 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 -1 54696p01.dat
0 STEP
